WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:03.448
This is research from
the Federal Modernization

3
00:00:03.448 --> 00:00:06.462
Workbook's Research section,
and it's still under review.

4
00:00:06.462 --> 00:00:09.017
When an agency retires Active Directory,

5
00:00:09.017 --> 00:00:14.051
two questions come up that nobody owned
before, because AD answered them quietly.

6
00:00:14.051 --> 00:00:15.762
Who gives a device its name?

7
00:00:15.762 --> 00:00:20.049
And who puts that name into DNS,
so other machines can find it?

8
00:00:20.049 --> 00:00:24.482
We'll look at the two jobs AD did at
once, who names each kind of device now,

9
00:00:24.482 --> 00:00:28.455
why devices can't register their
own names securely without AD,

10
00:00:28.455 --> 00:00:30.900
what takes over, and where nothing does.

11
00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:32.075
Then a checklist.

12
00:00:32.075 --> 00:00:36.337
Every fact comes from sources
opened on October twenty-second,

13
00:00:36.337 --> 00:00:40.763
2026, and I'll flag inferences and
program design guidance as we go.

14
00:00:40.763 --> 00:00:42.800
Let's start with what AD did.

15
00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:51.550
In Active Directory, a domain-joined
PC gets two jobs done almost for free.

16
00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:56.700
Job one is the name: a computer name plus
the domain's suffix makes its full name.

17
00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:58.594
Job two is the DNS record,

18
00:00:58.594 --> 00:01:03.138
the entries that map that name
to an address and back again.

19
00:01:03.138 --> 00:01:08.452
In AD, the zone lives on the domain
controllers and by default accepts only secure

20
00:01:08.452 --> 00:01:13.687
updates, signed with Kerberos, and once a
name exists only its owner can change it.

21
00:01:13.687 --> 00:01:16.701
The PC has a machine
account, so it can sign.

22
00:01:16.701 --> 00:01:19.075
It registers its own forward record,

23
00:01:19.075 --> 00:01:22.638
and the Windows DHCP server
registers the reverse one.

24
00:01:22.638 --> 00:01:27.980
When AD retires, both jobs need a new
owner, and the second one is harder.

25
00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:33.405
So who names the device now?

26
00:01:33.405 --> 00:01:36.693
The short answer: whatever
provisions it, at build time.

27
00:01:36.693 --> 00:01:38.930
For Windows that's usually Autopilot.

28
00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:42.545
Classic Autopilot with Entra
join has a name template,

29
00:01:42.545 --> 00:01:47.205
fifteen characters or fewer, using
the serial number or random digits.

30
00:01:47.205 --> 00:01:52.777
The newer device preparation has a template
too, but only for devices with device

31
00:01:52.777 --> 00:01:57.767
association, so, as an inference, Book
05's description of it needs a review.

32
00:01:57.767 --> 00:02:02.304
After enrollment, Intune can rename
corporate Windows PCs and Macs,

33
00:02:02.304 --> 00:02:04.329
though not hybrid-joined ones.

34
00:02:04.329 --> 00:02:07.930
Macs have no enrollment-time
template that this research found.

35
00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:13.230
Linux, Azure Arc servers and cloud VMs take
their names from their own build tools.

36
00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:16.690
Naming has an owner;
registering is the hard part.

37
00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:21.702
Here's the core problem.

38
00:02:21.702 --> 00:02:27.277
A PC joined only to Entra ID still
tries to register its name in DNS.

39
00:02:27.277 --> 00:02:30.966
A secure zone refuses its
unsigned attempt, as it should.

40
00:02:30.966 --> 00:02:35.803
And Microsoft documents that Windows
can use only Kerberos for secure update.

41
00:02:35.803 --> 00:02:39.690
So, as an inference, with no
machine account in the domain,

42
00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:43.577
the PC can't sign, and the zone
gets no record from it at all.

43
00:02:43.577 --> 00:02:47.290
The tempting fix is to let the
zone accept unsigned updates.

44
00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:47.978
Don't.

45
00:02:47.978 --> 00:02:50.677
Microsoft says that without secure update,

46
00:02:50.677 --> 00:02:54.277
any computer on the network
can modify an existing record.

47
00:02:54.277 --> 00:02:57.778
Remote clients over VPN
don't even try by default.

48
00:02:57.778 --> 00:03:01.750
So something else has to register
endpoints, and it has to sign.

49
00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:08.087
What takes over is the DHCP server.

50
00:03:08.087 --> 00:03:11.979
It hands every device its address
for a set time, called a lease,

51
00:03:11.979 --> 00:03:16.176
so it already talks to every device
and can register names for them.

52
00:03:16.176 --> 00:03:21.441
The device sends its name when it asks
for an address, and the DHCP server,

53
00:03:21.441 --> 00:03:26.975
set to always update DNS, signs the update
with a shared key or with Kerberos.

54
00:03:26.975 --> 00:03:32.300
On Infoblox, lock each zone so only
the DHCP servers may update it.

55
00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:37.101
Two safeguards help: the server writes
an ownership record beside each name,

56
00:03:37.101 --> 00:03:39.540
so only one client holds a name at a time,

57
00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:43.350
and it removes the records it
created when the lease expires.

58
00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:46.290
But ownership records
hide a migration trap.

59
00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:54.240
Ownership records only help if the
old and new systems agree on them.

60
00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:57.311
Infoblox has two modes,
Interim and Standard,

61
00:03:57.311 --> 00:04:00.715
and documents that checking
fails in three cases.

62
00:04:00.715 --> 00:04:04.606
The big one is migration from
Microsoft systems, where,

63
00:04:04.606 --> 00:04:09.015
in Infoblox's words, new DDNS
updates fail after the migration.

64
00:04:09.015 --> 00:04:12.525
The others are mixed DHCP
vendors and laptops whose

65
00:04:12.525 --> 00:04:15.790
wired and Wi-Fi interfaces
look like two clients.

66
00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:19.674
Macs add a twist: private Wi-Fi
addresses can rotate, and,

67
00:04:19.674 --> 00:04:24.852
as an inference, that changes the
identity the ownership record rests on.

68
00:04:24.852 --> 00:04:29.139
An MDM profile can turn it off
on macOS fifteen and later.

69
00:04:29.139 --> 00:04:33.479
The design guidance: pick one
mode once, migrate deliberately,

70
00:04:33.479 --> 00:04:37.052
and turn off address
randomization on agency Wi-Fi.

71
00:04:37.052 --> 00:04:39.350
Next, IPv6.

72
00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:46.525
IPv6 adds addresses that nobody registers.

73
00:04:46.525 --> 00:04:52.187
If a DHCPv6 server assigns the address,
it knows it and can register the record.

74
00:04:52.187 --> 00:04:56.970
But with SLAAC, the device builds its own
address from what the router announces,

75
00:04:56.970 --> 00:04:59.724
and creates temporary
privacy addresses too.

76
00:04:59.724 --> 00:05:03.166
As an inference, no server
ever learns those addresses,

77
00:05:03.166 --> 00:05:06.760
so no server-side registration
creates a record for them,

78
00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:10.813
and a post-AD Windows client can't
register them securely itself.

79
00:05:10.813 --> 00:05:15.900
The consequence, also an inference:
treat those records as absent by design.

80
00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:20.000
Find endpoints by their
DHCP-assigned addresses,

81
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:24.900
and track IPv6 activity from
DHCP and neighbor logs, not DNS.

82
00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:28.825
Whether Windows registers temporary
addresses couldn't be verified.

83
00:05:28.825 --> 00:05:30.700
Servers work differently.

84
00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:37.975
Servers and cloud machines don't
follow the endpoint pattern.

85
00:05:37.975 --> 00:05:43.107
On premises, servers with fixed addresses
get static records in the IPAM and DDI

86
00:05:43.107 --> 00:05:45.522
system, and, as program design guidance,

87
00:05:45.522 --> 00:05:49.975
the pipeline that builds a server
should create and delete its records.

88
00:05:49.975 --> 00:05:53.705
Linux servers in an identity
domain can sign their own updates,

89
00:05:53.705 --> 00:05:58.512
but watch for a fallback to unauthenticated
updates if signing fails at install.

90
00:05:58.512 --> 00:06:04.240
In Azure, private DNS autoregistration
does the job, with real limits: VMs only,

91
00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:07.909
forward records only, and
records that disappear

92
00:06:07.909 --> 00:06:11.488
when the VM is stopped or
the network is unlinked.

93
00:06:11.488 --> 00:06:15.277
In AWS, this research found
no built-in registration

94
00:06:15.277 --> 00:06:18.362
into your own zones, so
you build a pipeline.

95
00:06:18.362 --> 00:06:20.560
Now, remote workers.

96
00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:29.097
Remote clients are a different story: they
find names, but they don't register them.

97
00:06:29.097 --> 00:06:31.249
With Microsoft's Global Secure Access,

98
00:06:31.249 --> 00:06:35.886
the laptop's client sends matching queries
through Microsoft's edge to a connector

99
00:06:35.886 --> 00:06:37.028
inside your network,

100
00:06:37.028 --> 00:06:40.523
and gets back a stand-in address
that routes through the tunnel.

101
00:06:40.523 --> 00:06:46.522
But no primary source found says it registers
the laptop's own name in agency DNS.

102
00:06:46.522 --> 00:06:52.122
So, as an inference, remote clients are
resolvers of names, not registrants.

103
00:06:52.122 --> 00:06:55.398
That changes how the help
desk finds a remote device.

104
00:06:55.398 --> 00:06:58.647
Intune's Remote Help starts
from the device list,

105
00:06:58.647 --> 00:07:02.384
with no DNS lookup, but you
pick by the Intune device name.

106
00:07:02.384 --> 00:07:06.420
As an inference, that name
must be unique and meaningful.

107
00:07:10.170 --> 00:07:14.170
If devices sign in with cloud
tokens, why do names still matter?

108
00:07:14.170 --> 00:07:15.195
Four reasons.

109
00:07:15.195 --> 00:07:20.855
First, Kerberos islands: an Entra-joined
device reaching on-premises resources still

110
00:07:20.855 --> 00:07:25.080
finds domain controllers through
DNS, and Book 08 explains that

111
00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:29.145
a name pointing at the wrong host
gets a valid ticket rejected.

112
00:07:29.145 --> 00:07:33.374
Second, certificates bind a name
to a key, so, as an inference,

113
00:07:33.374 --> 00:07:38.572
after AD the certificate's name has
to be built from the device name plus

114
00:07:38.572 --> 00:07:43.770
a fixed agency suffix, and it only
matches DNS if that name is registered.

115
00:07:43.770 --> 00:07:48.127
Third, logs: Book 12 joins
evidence on the device's identity,

116
00:07:48.127 --> 00:07:51.757
so a missing or duplicated
name breaks that join.

117
00:07:51.757 --> 00:07:57.100
Fourth, Microsoft says its naming
macros don't guarantee unique names.

118
00:08:00.850 --> 00:08:05.038
Here's the whole picture on one page,
with the full version in the backup.

119
00:08:05.038 --> 00:08:08.504
Entra-joined Windows is
named by Autopilot or Intune

120
00:08:08.504 --> 00:08:12.050
and registered by the DHCP
server with signed updates.

121
00:08:12.050 --> 00:08:16.100
Hybrid-joined Windows keeps working
through AD until it's rebuilt.

122
00:08:16.100 --> 00:08:17.957
Macs are renamed in Intune,

123
00:08:17.957 --> 00:08:21.925
and DHCP should register them,
though that's an inference.

124
00:08:21.925 --> 00:08:26.087
Linux is registered by DHCP
unless it signs its own updates.

125
00:08:26.087 --> 00:08:28.829
On-premises servers get static records,

126
00:08:28.829 --> 00:08:34.063
Azure VMs are autoregistered within
limits, and AWS needs your own pipeline.

127
00:08:34.063 --> 00:08:37.857
And printers and other unmanaged
devices get names from a

128
00:08:37.857 --> 00:08:42.125
reservation or the device itself,
with DHCP registering for them.

129
00:08:42.125 --> 00:08:44.640
Now, what the agency must do.

130
00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:52.825
Here's what the agency must do, condensed
from a seventeen-step checklist,

131
00:08:52.825 --> 00:08:55.265
and all of it is program design guidance.

132
00:08:55.265 --> 00:09:01.069
Before AD DNS retires, choose one naming
authority per platform, make the DHCP

133
00:09:01.069 --> 00:09:02.792
server the registrant,

134
00:09:02.792 --> 00:09:07.327
and lock each zone so only the
DHCP servers' keys can update it.

135
00:09:07.327 --> 00:09:09.754
Pick one ownership-record format,

136
00:09:09.754 --> 00:09:14.953
turn off address randomization on
agency Wi-Fi, and create reverse zones.

137
00:09:14.953 --> 00:09:18.988
At cutover, deliver DNS suffixes
through DHCP and Intune,

138
00:09:18.988 --> 00:09:23.710
and for Entra-joined Windows,
either turn client registration off

139
00:09:23.710 --> 00:09:28.603
or accept refused attempts, but
never open zones to unsigned updates.

140
00:09:28.603 --> 00:09:31.910
Afterward, turn on cleanup
only after a quiet period,

141
00:09:31.910 --> 00:09:35.490
and monitor duplicates and
devices with no record.

142
00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:40.165
To close.

143
00:09:40.165 --> 00:09:43.651
The decisions are which tool
names each kind of device,

144
00:09:43.651 --> 00:09:47.835
the DHCP server as registrant and
which keys may update each zone,

145
00:09:47.835 --> 00:09:53.103
which ownership-record format, and whether
Entra-joined PCs try to register at all.

146
00:09:53.103 --> 00:09:57.914
The next steps are the things that couldn't
be verified, such as how Macs handle

147
00:09:57.914 --> 00:10:02.877
rename and DHCP names, and a recheck of
Book 05's device preparation description.

148
00:10:02.877 --> 00:10:07.778
Ask any third-party VPN vendor whether
its concentrator registers clients.

149
00:10:07.778 --> 00:10:12.951
In the workbook, Book 05 covers
onboarding and naming, Book 08 covers

150
00:10:12.951 --> 00:10:18.402
the AD DNS departure, and two related
research items sit beside this one.

151
00:10:18.402 --> 00:10:23.327
This is research under review, not an
endorsement by any federal agency.

152
00:10:23.327 --> 00:10:24.140
Thank you.
